# Freeze the Gmail-parity MCP contract

## Goal

Define a stable provider-neutral tool surface before transport and adapter code
couple behavior to one implementation.

## Completed work

- Froze 29 public tool/capability names as `mail-tools/v1`.
- Defined account-scoped opaque identifiers for messages, threads, mailboxes,
  labels, drafts, senders, attachments, and event cursors.
- Defined structured search, UTC time semantics, snapshot pagination, stable
  ordering, cursor expiry, page limits, and bounded change waiting.
- Defined verified sender selection and rejected free-form From substitution.
- Defined separate draft, send, reply, reply-all, forward, read-state, label,
  archive, move, trash, restore, and destructive operations.
- Defined batch bounds, per-item results, attachment bounds, idempotency keys,
  optimistic draft versions, and ambiguous-send behavior.
- Defined a stable structured error vocabulary with retry classes.
- Froze all four MCP annotation hints while keeping server authorization
  independent from those hints.
- Documented Gmail-like compatibility with direct-protocol evidence required
  before capability advertisement.
- Added a machine-readable golden JSON fixture and serialization round-trip
  test.

## Acceptance evidence

- `src/mail/capability/contract/v1.rs` owns the serialized contract boundary.
- `docs/contract/tool-contract-v1.mdc` defines schema semantics and
  compatibility.
- `docs/contract/tool-contract-v1.json` is the golden machine-readable fixture.
- Contract and `planned_capabilities()` inventories are asserted identical.
- The golden contract serializes, deserializes, and byte-compares
  deterministically.
- Permanent deletion remains disabled by default and has no `ActionGrant`.
- Targeted contract tests, strict Clippy, and Jig complete cleanly.
